meta {
  name: Test
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/test?message=Hi Subscriber
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  message: Hi Subscriber
  ~chatId: @mychannel
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  # Notifications — Test

  Sends a TEST notification on demand so you can verify token / chat id /
  connectivity without waiting for a real trade.

  ## Query
  - `message` — optional custom text (default "Test alert from the trading bot ✅").
  - `chatId`  — optional. When set, the message is sent to THIS chat/channel id
    using the shared bot token — handy to verify a GROUP or a separate channel
    (e.g. the swift-scan channel) before going live. Accepts:
      - `@channelname` — a public channel/group
      - `-100...`      — a private channel/supergroup id
      - a numeric user id
    When omitted, the configured main notifier is used.

  ## Response (200)
  { target, notifierEnabled?, sent, note/hint, timestamp }
  - With `chatId`: target = "chatId <id>", sent=true means a send was attempted
    via the bot token. If it didn't arrive, check app logs for a
    [TelegramChannelSender] warning — the usual cause for a group/channel is the
    bot not being a MEMBER/ADMIN of it.
  - Without `chatId`: notifierEnabled=false means notifications are off or not
    fully configured (webull.notifications.enabled=true, channel=TELEGRAM, bot
    token + chat id). sent=true → check the main chat; failures log a
    [TelegramNotifier] warning.

  ## Setup reminder
  - notifications.enabled: true
  - env: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN (shared), TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID (main chat)
  - For a group/channel: add the bot to it and make it an admin, then test with
    ?chatId=@yourchannel (or the -100... id).
}
